  "query_preview": "..\nThis morning's radar shows rain showers with embedded\nthunderstorms from Tucson and east. This will continue through\nthe morning as the low pressure center, located by Yuma, will\nmove east and north, pushing the main moisture to the northeast\ntowards the White Mountains. By sunrise, there will be a brief \nlull in activity before recharging again in the afternoon and \nthe remaining atmospheric moisture within the system. The best \nchances for afternoon thunderstorms will be in Graham and \nGreenlee Counties, especially in the mountains and along the New\nMexico state line. Rainfall amounts will generally be up to a \ntenth with pockets of higher amounts of 0.25 to 0.35 inches. \nWith those low amounts, there is a 10 percent of dry lightning \nin the afternoon. Storm chances will rapidly drop off tonight, \nreturning to dry conditions for the remainder of the week. \n\nThe low pressure system will also bring some breezes across the\nregion starting this afternoon as the pressure gradient slowly\ntightens. Then, our next weather maker will move into the Great\nBasin region over the next two days. The ensemble models have \nshifted the position of the center to into California than over \nNevada in the previous model runs; making the pressure gradient\nis not as tight as previously, but it will still bring breezy \nto locally windy conditions across the region. Coupled with the \ndry conditions, there will be elevated fire weather conditions \non Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nOverall temperatures will be hovering near normal for the most\nof the week. Ensemble models are pointing to ideas of a ridge\nbuilding to our east, but different solutions on the location of\nthe ridge axis. Either solution, temperatures will be trending \nup to above normal by next weekend.",
